General notes (apply to all points)
To be marked as correct, they don't have to have used specific keywords, (e.g. they don't have to say 'inductive
hypothesis' while stating the inductive hypothesis, or 'base case' while showing the base case)
For ease of consistency, don't mark of any points for statements that may seem out of order.
If a chain of equalities is correct, but seems logically out of order, the rubric point can still be marked 'c'
A header for a rubric point, e.g. 'Base Case:' or 'Inductive Hypothesis:', would not receive any credit on its own, it
must be followed by an actual attempt at completing that rubric point.
If inductive hypothesis bound and inductive step goal don't match, goal should be marked as 'p'. Inductive hypothesis
bound may still be marked as 'c' in this case, because at the point when its stated, it isn't wrong.
